        {
          "id": "u11l2",
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Il presente dei verbi in -are",
          "dictionaryRefs": [
            "parlare",
            "mangiare",
            "abitare",
            "lavorare",
            "studiare"
          ],
          "grammarNotes": "I VERBI IN -ARE — the biggest, most regular family of Italian verbs. Verbs ending in -are are (almost) all regular: drop -are, add the six endings.\n\nPARLARE (to speak) — the full present tense:\n\nio parlo — I speak\ntu parli — you speak\nlui/lei parla — he/she speaks\nnoi parliamo — we speak\nvoi parlate — you speak (plural)\nloro parlano — they speak\n\nTHE SIX ENDINGS: -o, -i, -a, -iamo, -ate, -ano. Learn them as a rhythm: O - I - A - IAMO - ATE - ANO.\n\nThe same endings work for every regular -are verb:\n• MANGIARE (to eat): mangio, mangi, mangia, mangiamo, mangiate, mangiano\n• ABITARE (to live): abito, abiti, abita, abitiamo, abitate, abitano\n• LAVORARE (to work, Unit 8): lavoro, lavori, lavora, lavoriamo, lavorate, lavorano\n• STUDIARE (to study, Unit 8): studio, studi, studia, studiamo, studiate, studiano\n• ASCOLTARE (to listen): ascolto, ascolti, ascolta, ascoltiamo, ascoltate, ascoltano\n\nWHY YOU CAN DROP THE SUBJECT: 'Parliamo italiano.' — only noi can take -iamo, so 'noi' is unnecessary. Italians say the pronoun only for emphasis or contrast: 'Io parlo italiano, tu parli inglese.'\n\nWATCH OUT: fare, andare, venire (Unit 7) end in -are/-re but are irregular — their forms stay as you learned them.",
          "exercises": [
            {
              "id": "u11l2e1",
              "type": "fill_blank",
              "prompt": "Complete the sentence. (You speak Italian well.)",
              "sentence": "Tu ___ bene l'italiano.",
