          ],
          "grammarNotes": "AL RISTORANTE — ordering politely.\n\nVOLERE (to want) — the full present:\n\nio voglio — I want\ntu vuoi — you want\nlui/lei vuole — he/she wants\nnoi vogliamo — we want\nvoi volete — you want (plural)\nloro vogliono — they want\n\n'Voglio una pizza!' — direct. Fine with friends and family; blunt with a waiter.\n\n'VORREI' — I would like (Unit 9's set phrase): same meaning as voglio, much softer.\n'Vorrei un tavolo per due.' — I would like a table for two.\nAt a restaurant, vorrei is the standard. (Its grammar — the conditional — arrives in Unit 41.)\n\nTHREE WAYS TO ORDER:\n• 'Vorrei il pesce.' — I would like the fish. (polite classic)\n• 'Prendo la pasta.' — I'll have the pasta. (prendere, Unit 12)\n• 'Per me il vino.' — For me, the wine. (when everyone orders in turn)\n\nARTICLES WITH FOOD: for a dish from the menu, use il/la — 'Prendo il pesce', 'Per me la pasta'. For one item, use un/una — 'Un vino, per favore', 'Un'acqua minerale'.\n\nTHE ITALIAN DAY — culture note:\n• la colazione — breakfast: cappuccino e cornetto, standing at the bar (Unit 9)\n• il pranzo — lunch, around 13:00\n• la cena — dinner, late: 20:00 or later. Nobody eats dinner at six.\nAt the restaurant: il primo (a pasta), il secondo (meat or fish)… and to finish, 'Il conto, per favore.' (Unit 9).\n• 'Buon appetito!' — enjoy your meal, said when the food arrives.",
