          "grammarNotes": "A, IN, DA — three little words that do a lot.\n\nA — to/at a city or small place: 'Vado a Roma.', 'Vado al bar.' (a + il → al; a + la → alla: 'Vado alla stazione.')\nIN — to/in a country or region, and 'by' + transport: 'Vado in Italia.', 'Abito in Italia.', 'in treno' (by train).\nDA — from, or 'to someone's place': 'Vengo da Milano.' (Unit 7), 'Vado da Giulia.' — I go to Giulia's.\n\nWHY a vs in: cities take A (a Roma, a Milano); countries take IN (in Italia, in Francia). Small = a, big = in.\n\nL'IMPERATIVO (tu, informal) — telling one person you know what to do:\n• -are verbs: -a! — 'Gira a destra!' (Turn right!) The tu vowel flips: tu giri → gira!\n• -ere/-ire verbs: -i! — same as the present: 'Prendi la prima via a sinistra.' (Take the first street on the left.)\n• andare is irregular: vai! — 'Vai dritto!' (Go straight!)\n\nDIRECTIONS SET PHRASES:\n• 'Dov'è la stazione?' — Where is the station? (dove + è → dov'è)\n• 'È qui vicino.' — It's near here. (vicino = near)\n• 'Grazie mille!' — Thanks a lot! (literally: a thousand thanks)",
