          ],
          "grammarNotes": "I COLORI — colors are adjectives, and adjectives agree (Unit 13). This lesson is that rule at full speed.\n\nTHREE FAMILIES OF COLOR:\n• -o family (four forms): rosso/rossa/rossi/rosse, nero/nera/neri/nere, bianco/bianca/bianchi/bianchi… wait — bianche! (f pl). Also giallo, grigio.\n• -e family (two forms): verde/verdi — like intelligente.\n• INVARIABLE (never change): blu, rosa, viola. 'Un vestito blu, due vestiti blu.'\n\nPOSITION: the color comes AFTER the noun: 'una maglietta rossa' — a red t-shirt; 'le scarpe nere' — the black shoes.\n\nTHE CLOTHES:\n• la maglietta (f) — the t-shirt\n• i pantaloni (m pl) — the trousers (always plural, like 'pants')\n• le scarpe (f pl) — the shoes (always a pair)\n• il vestito (m) — the dress; also a suit\n• la giacca (f) — the jacket\n\n💛 COMPLIMENTING WHAT SOMEONE WEARS:\n• 'Che bella maglietta!' — What a nice t-shirt! (che + adjective + noun; bella agrees with maglietta)\n• 'Mi piace la tua giacca.' — I like your jacket. (Unit 20 + the possessive, Unit 14 — clothes keep the article!)\n• 'Hai delle scarpe bellissime'? — not yet; for now: 'Le tue scarpe sono belle.' — Your shoes are beautiful.",
