          ],
          "grammarNotes": "STARE vs essere — two verbs for 'to be', two different jobs.\n\nSTARE (health, mood, how things are going) — the full present:\n\nio sto — tu stai — lui/lei sta — noi stiamo — voi state — loro stanno\n\n• 'Come stai?' — How are you? — 'Sto bene, grazie.' / 'Sto male.' (I'm unwell) / 'Così così.' (so-so)\n• Health uses stare: 'Non sto bene oggi.' — I'm not well today.\n• essere stays for WHO/WHAT you are: 'Sono Alex.', 'Sono americano.', 'Sono stanco.' — identity, origin, states.\n\nWHY the split: stare asks about your CONDITION right now; essere says what you ARE. Compare the two questions you know: 'Come stai?' (how are you feeling) vs 'Com'è?' (Unit 13: what is he/she like).\n\nMAL DI… — aches with avere: 'Ho mal di testa.' — I have a headache. 'Ho mal di denti.' — I have a toothache (i denti = the teeth). The pattern: ho + mal di + body part.\n\nPREVIEW — set phrase only, real grammar in Stage 6: stare + -ando/-endo = happening right now. 'Sto lavorando.' — I'm working (right now). 'Sto mangiando.' — I'm eating. For now, learn 'Sto bene' solid.",
